<ecoSpold xmlns="http://www.EcoInvent.org/EcoSpold02">
  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="20fe745e-75de-5df4-85b8-b4c1825b16e7" activityNameId="f7d6e5aa-7945-520d-afb8-b5e0a07243a8" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Hard coal, at mine</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Hard coal, at mine
Update 2022 by ZHAW only updated methane emissions at mine, Remark: Specific items for this CN dataset are: electricity supply from local coal power plant and grid (CN) (averages for Shanxi Province); blasting, steel, and wood requirements (averages for Shanxi); emission of methane (China avarege); emission of CO2 and SO2 from uncontrolled coal fires (estimated country-wide). Other data have been taken from the dataset CPA. Average data for coal mining in the CPA region are from a summary study on material flows and energy uses. Data are normalized by the mass unit of produced coal, including preparation and without differentiating steam coal from the rest. In order to enable a consistent calculation of the cumulative energy demand, the extracted mass of raw coal was adjusted by a factor of 1.03, taking into account the difference in the upper heating value of raw coal of this region (19.7 MJ/kg), compared to the used worldwide average of 19.1 MJ/kg. Mine reclamation is not included in this module, because it mostly concerns the tailings, which are described in a separate module. Electricity, heating from heavy oil industrial boilers, and diesel uses are included. The use of the module "heat, at hard coal industrial furnace 1-10MW" leads to an underestimation of emissions, because the quality of coal burned at mines is usually lower than coal burned in industrial boilers elsewhere. Water uses in mining and coal washing have been approximately described using the module "tap water". Particle emissions are assumed to be all greater than 10 micrometer. Radon emissions are recalculated from average value worldwide using the ratio of U content 36/20 from UNSCEAR 1993. Emissions to water are estimated for Western European conditions and applied to this region. They are assumed to be released to rivers. Possible emissions to groundwater have not been accounted for. All coalbed methane released from the seam is accounted for as natural gas resource and assumed to be entirely emitted to air. The methane recovered in some major mines, possibly used for own energetic needs and/or district heating, has not been described as an energy byproduct. The unaccounted energy is most likely negligible compared to the energy content of the product coal. An amount of coal corresponding to the assumed losses for spontaneous fires has been added to the coal resources used up.; NA; UUID: 5a85ec8a-0bd9-3316-95fa-d68a40f47846
Included processes: The dataset is based on "hard coal at mine" CPA. The generic mine CPA dataset quantifies the directly affected area and occupation during operation of the mine. Electricity, heat, and diesel requirements for mining operation are included. Pumped groundwater, explosives, emissions to air and water, and solid waste complete the module. Not included is recovered methane. Specific processes for this CN dataset are: electricity supply from local coal power plant and grid (CN); blasting, steel, and wood requirements.
Location:  Geography: Region specific (Shanxi Province and county-wide) with integration from generic mine.
Technology: Technology: Not specified.
Time period: 
Production volume: 
Sampling: Sampling procedure: literature
Extrapolations: Extrapolations: see Geography/Text
Uncertainty adjustments: none
Uncertainty adjustments: none
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_5a85ec8a-0bd9-3316-95fa-d68a40f47846.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
